In the golden age of Hollywood, the swimming pool was more than a place to cool off. It was a stage — a shimmering rectangle of turquoise where deals were brokered, romances kindled, and reputations made or shattered. The pools of Classic Hollywood were designed to impress, and seven decades later, many of them are still open to visitors willing to follow the trail of chlorine and celebrity.

## The Pools That Made History

No pool in America carries more legend than the Neptune Pool at Hearst Castle in San Simeon. Perched on a hilltop overlooking the Pacific, William Randolph Hearst's Roman-temple fantasy pool hosted Marion Davies, Charlie Chaplin, Cary Grant, and seemingly every star who mattered in the 1930s and '40s. Today you can tour the estate and stand at the pool's edge, gazing out at the same coastline view that once greeted the most powerful people in entertainment. The pool was actually built three times — Hearst kept demanding something grander — and the final version, completed in 1936, remains one of the most photographed bodies of water in the world.

> "The pool at the Beverly Hills Hotel isn't just a swimming pool. It's a state of mind — a place where you become, however briefly, part of the mythology." — poolside regular and columnist Sidney Skolsky

Closer to the heart of the industry, the pool at the Beverly Hills Hotel has been the centre of Hollywood social life since the 1940s. Known simply as "the pool," its cabanas were where agents like Swifty Lazar held court, where Katharine Hepburn once dove in fully clothed, and where Faye Dunaway was famously photographed the morning after winning her Oscar. The cabanas can still be reserved today, and while the prices have climbed considerably since the Rat Pack era, the experience of lounging poolside at the Pink Palace remains one of the great Hollywood pilgrimages.

Then there are the pools that have vanished into legend — the pool at the Garden of Allah on Sunset Boulevard, where F. Scott Fitzgerald, Dorothy Parker, and Humphrey Bogart drank more than they swam. The hotel was demolished in 1959, and a strip mall stands in its place, but a small plaque marks where it once stood. For the devoted Old Hollywood traveller, even the ghost of a great pool is worth a visit.
